Subject: [PATCH] ctrl: the radio count of a model built from the network initialised, the radio array bounded

em_ctrl crashed (SIGSEGV) when a new agent onboarded for the first time: in one lab
(rdk-1004, 8 October 2026) a Raspberry Pi pod's agent started, the controller answered with a
Backhaul STA Capability Query and died in dm_radio_t::operator= under
dm_easy_mesh_t::operator= (dm_easy_mesh.cpp:73), em_cmd_t::init, the em_cmd_bsta_cap_t
constructor and dm_easy_mesh_ctrl_t::analyze_bsta_cap_req, reading past the top of its stack.

analyze_bsta_cap_req (and the two other analyze functions that start with
dm_easy_mesh_t dm = *this) builds its model through dm_easy_mesh_t(const dm_network_t&), the
controller being a dm_network_t. That constructor set only m_wifi_data, so m_num_radios and the
other counts held whatever the stack held. em_cmd_t::init then copied the model, and
operator= copied obj.m_num_radios radios into m_radio[EM_MAX_BANDS], however many that was.

The constructor now delegates to the default one, which zeroes the counts. operator= copies at
most EM_MAX_BANDS radios. Every place that appends a radio (the two Radio Basic Capability
handlers, put_radio, commit_config's new radio, the em_insert orchestration, the RadioList
decode, the policy request and the radio-enable target) checks the array first and logs what
it drops; the policy request's guard was EM_MAX_RADIO_PER_AGENT (4) against an array of
EM_MAX_BANDS (3). Platform-independent; a candidate for upstream.

Upstream-Status: Pending

